From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 09:56:33 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 30/62] hjviewer: same-account login: the account module,
 account requests and key stores

The base of 1.6.0's same-account logins, on which the PC's side and the
controller's side build (design v3 3-2, 3-3, 3-6, 4-1, 4-2, 4-4, 4-7, 6-3,
6-5): the protocol's fields, the account module with what every platform
shares, and the account requests with the account key's stores.

The common part:

- message.proto: LoginRequest.hjv_key_proof (HjvKeyProof: version, pk,
  signature, key_serial) and Hash.hjv_nonce, numbered 1001 to stay clear of
  upstream's fields. Empty, they encode to nothing: a Hash without a nonce is
  byte for byte 1.5.1's, and a 1.5.1 peer skips both as unknown fields.
- src/hjviewer_account/mod.rs: the build variables HJV_LOGIN_SEAL_PUBKEY and
  HJV_TRUST_PUBKEYS and enabled(), true only with HJV_SERVER and both keys
  (an account build); the context strings hjv-ckey-v1, hjv-req-v1,
  hjv-conn-v1, hjv-pc-v1 and hjv-trust-v1 and the bytes each signature
  covers; the connection kinds; MAX_LIST_TTL (72 h) and
  MIN_REFRESH/MAX_REFRESH (5-300 s); the PC's checks of a trust list and of a
  proof (verify_list, valid_at, check_key_login, should_refresh_for). The
  proof's signature is checked before the list is looked at, so a forged
  proof never makes the PC call hjv-api. The names of the service's options
  by which the UI asks the PC's sync task (hjv-bind-req, hjv-unbind-req,
  hjv-pc-sync) are here once, for the account requests that write them and
  the service that reads them.
- pc.rs and ctl.rs: the names and types the PC's and the controller's hooks
  call, doing nothing yet. pc.rs is compiled on desktops only; phones get the
  same names from mod.rs, doing nothing.
- vectors.rs: fixed-seed vectors shared with hjv-api (a trust list, a
  connection proof, two request proofs, a sync envelope with its use report,
  the sealed login's ck and cp), computed with ed25519-dalek and reproduced
  here on libsodium. json() renders the copy hjv-api's tests read.

The account requests: in an account build the app's account requests, which
go through Rust's http_request there, are handled in api.rs:

- The first step of a password login is sealed to hjv-api's key (seal.rs's
  format) with a new account key's public half and proof (ck, cp) inside the
  seal, and the device key signs it (devsig.rs's format, with hjv.installed
  and the app's version). The key is kept only when the answer brings a
  token; the code step of a two-factor login goes as it is.
- Every request with a token carries X-HJV-Proof, made in one place,
  proof_header(), which the minimum version work's update check calls too. A
  token goes only to the account paths, signed; with no account key (a
  restored iPhone) it is dropped with a log-in-again notice, and while the
  key store is locked nothing is sent: http_request answers with an error
  instead, which the app does not take as a logout.
- The answers: the server's clock (a 409 clock answer's server_time, else the
  Date header) shifts the t of the next signature and seal, kept in
  LocalConfig hjv-clock-offset when it moved by more than 30 s; a login's
  hjv.ks, and its hjv.binding and hjv.login_at, which become this user's
  hjv-pc-binding and the service's bind request (Config2 hjv-bind-req) with
  a knock (hjv-pc-sync); /api/peers fills hjv-my-devices (per user, the PCs'
  keys, states and kinds), replaced only by a complete 200 list put together
  page by page, and only while the account key is there, so a page that
  comes after an explicit logout does not bring the list back. Failures,
  401, 400, 409 and the app's own logout keep it; only an explicit logout and
  another account's login drop it.
- An explicit logout (POST /api/logout) is signed, then, before the request
  goes out, drops the key and the list and, where this user's login bound
  the PC, puts the unbind request in for the bind request and a knock in one
  write of the service's options, so the PC stops taking proofs whether or
  not the logout arrives. Taking this PC out of the list again (POST
  /api/hjv/this-device/undo answered removed) unbinds the same way.
- main_get_common/main_set_common answer the account screens (hjv-account,
  hjv-relogin, hjv-pc-bound), and other PCs keep seeing the OS user name, not
  the login's name.

The account key lives in the iPhone's Keychain (when unlocked, this device
only), on Windows in a DPAPI-wrapped file in %LOCALAPPDATA%\HJViewer-account,
outside the folder the portable packer clears for every new version, and
elsewhere in LocalConfig hjv-ctl-key. Android keeps the app's data out of
cloud backups and device transfers; the iOS privacy manifest lists the
portal ID and name hjv-api keeps.

Hooks: lib.rs (the module), ui_interface.rs http_request (H13, H14),
common.rs (H15: account requests never retry with an invalid certificate),
flutter_ffi.rs (H16), hjviewer.rs (H17: an account build disables the
address book instead of the account and fixes enable-flutter-http-on-rust;
pinned() is pub(crate) for enabled()), client.rs (H21). Cargo.toml:
security-framework on iOS, the Win32_Security_Cryptography feature.

A build without the three variables keeps 1.5.1's paths: no nonce, no proof,
every hook returns at once.

Tests (cargo test --lib hjviewer_account): the list and proof rules including
the security review's two poison lists, the sync interval for a refresh of 0,
1, 3600 and u64::MAX, a bad signature never asking for a sync, the vectors,
the Hash of builds missing one of the three variables against a 1.5.1
parser and the bytes 1.5.1 sends, and a 1.5.1 parser reading a LoginRequest
with a proof; the seal and its payload against the shared vectors, the code
step left alone, X-HJV-Proof against the shared vectors and hjv-api's README
vectors of the app's device list and logout requests, no token unsigned or
with a locked store, the bind request, the logout's one write before the
request (also offline, locked or without a token) and a 401 or 400 changing
nothing, hjv-my-devices across pages, failures, a new process and a page
answered after the logout, the clock offset, strict TLS for account paths,
the account screens' queries, the name other PCs see, the Windows key folder
apart from the packer's, and the hooks doing nothing in a build without the
feature.
The hooks in the shared files themselves (H13's error, H15, H21) are the
account E2E's to exercise.

Known limits: a login answer without a binding (another account's login on
this PC after the app's own logout) leaves this user's hjv-pc-binding, so a
later explicit logout releases that PC's earlier binding, which, like the
off switch, opens nothing. The logout's write of the service's options is
not confirmed: when the service cannot be reached at that moment and the
logout does not reach hjv-api either, the PC keeps its binding. A device list
asked for before a logout and answered only after another account's login is
kept as that account's until its own list comes; a proof it sends to such a
PC meanwhile is refused there.

(Design v3, work packages C0 and C1: patch 0030.)

